From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] glm cuda: TF_GLM_OVN_PIECE_FRONT, _MLA and _FFN, the next block's row-by-row front run piece by piece as the rows land

A prompt chunk's pieced site ends in a tail: the last piece's exchange, its glue and its share run on the side stream while the
main stream waits, and only then does the next block start with its row-by-row front (for a KDA layer one 4.2 ms matmul over the
whole chunk, for a DSA layer the projections, norms, indexer and absorbed query, for a MoE ffn the router and top-k pick). On the
GB10 triangle the wait is about 2.5 ms a site, 45 sites a 4,096-row pass, 9.3% of a cold pass (queue76's nsys timeline).

TF_GLM_OVN_PIECE_FRONT=1 changes an ffn site that a KDA layer follows, and TF_GLM_OVN_PIECE_MLA=1 one that a DSA layer follows.
The site's pieces are filled on the main stream as before; each piece's exchange, glue and share then run on the side stream in
that order (cuda_forward_pair.exchangeGlue, the paired chunk's schedule, now public), so a piece's glued rows are in after its own
share instead of after the last piece's. The main stream then runs the next layer's front range by range, each behind its own
`p.shared[q]` (cuda_forward_front.mixer): for a KDA layer cuda_kda_block.projectRows (project over rows lo..hi, into those rows of
s.p, s.ka and s.kg), for a DSA layer cuda_dsa_block.prepareRows (the projection, the two norms, q_b, the indexer's keys and gate,
the absorbed query and the indexer's query for rows lo..hi, each into its own buffer at that buffer's row stride, as Scratch
documents them). The layer's block then continues from its chain (cuda_kda_block.bodyRest) or its attention
(cuda_dsa_block.bodyRest); bodyProfile and body call those for the same steps.

TF_GLM_OVN_PIECE_FFN=1 changes a mixer site that a MoE ffn follows. The glue stays on the main stream (the front is a router of
0.7 ms, short against the side stream's chain, so moving the glue would only add to it); the closing waits of gluePieces are split
off (gluePiecesAsync returns the mask of pieces whose shares are in flight, awaitGlued waits for them) and the router and top-k pick
(cuda_moe.routeRows: glue.router and glue.select over rows lo..hi, into those rows of mlog, pick and wts) run range by range behind
each range's share; the block continues from its plan (cuda_moe.bodyRest, which body now calls for the same two steps).

TF_GLM_OVN_FRONT_ROWS=N (default 0) lets a front range join pieces until it has N rows (a small chunk's pieces re-read a front's weights once a piece),
the bits the same. A front runs on the pieces as cut except that a last piece under 128 rows joins the one before it (cuda_forward_pieces.frontRanges),
so no front matmul or router sees fewer rows than a block (a short matmul can take another kernel than the whole chunk's). A range's
rows are the same rows through the same kernels, K slices fixed by the shape, so the bits are the same. An ffn site before the last
layer, a dense ffn, a profiled pass and a shared window's segments keep today's schedule (cuda_forward_pieces.handsFront).
Default off: with the three switches unset nothing launches differently.

Host test: the switches, the hand-over rule and the ranges (cuda_piece_front_test.zig, listed in cuda.zig's test block). The
registry rows and the docs lines follow TF_GLM_OVN_SIDE_PRIO's. The engine logs the switches at boot. Not run on a GPU yet.

New here, not a re-seat: written on this series at v1.2.9.

